Story.register = {
	style = "ledger",
	text = [[MARROW LODGE — GUESTS

June 3, 1951 — The Hallorans, St. Paul.
"Sunsets like nothing else!"

June 4, 1951 — The Hallorans.
"Another lovely sunset."

June 4, 1951 — The Hallorans.
"Same one?"

Aug. 19, 1977 — R. Pike.
"Mr. Marrow a gracious host. Never saw him leave the house. Heard his keys all night."

Sept. 9, 1989 — Dana & Theo Okafor.
"Arrived Friday. It still isn't Saturday."

Sept. 9, 1989 — Theo.
"front door won't open"]],
}

Story.odetteLetter = {
	style = "hand",
	text = [[Margaret —

The lodge is finished at last. Ansel cut every lock himself, and there is a single key that answers to all of them. He wears it on a cord around his neck, even to sleep. When I asked for one of my own he only smiled and said a house should have one keeper.

June adores it here. She sits on the back steps every evening to watch the sun go down behind the pines, and every evening I must call her in twice.

Come in August. The light here is like honey.

— Odette
June, 1932]],
}

Story.journal1 = {
	style = "hand",
	text = [[October 14th, 1934.

June went out to the yard at twenty to seven to watch the sun go down. She did not come in.

I walked the pines with the lantern until it burned dry. Odette has not stopped crying.

I have stopped every clock in the house at twenty to seven. If the evening does not end, then she has not been gone a night. She is only late.]],
}

Story.journal2 = {
	style = "hand",
	text = [[Undated.

The sun has not moved. I have stopped asking it to.

Odette says I have turned the house into a lock. She is not wrong. Every door here answers to my hand, and the woods cannot take what the doors keep.

The walls are another matter. I never told her about the narrow way behind her green book, the one she never finished reading. I built it for June, for hide-and-seek.

June always found it first.]],
}

Story.odetteUnsent = {
	style = "hand",
	text = [[Ansel nailed the coal chute shut today.

June used to climb out of it into the yard, black with soot and laughing, and he could not stand that there was a way out of this house that did not go through his key.

He carried the pry bar up to the attic so she could never reach it again. She is gone, and still he keeps it up there.

I am going to find the bar. I am going to leave the way she did.]],
}

